WHAT ARE FUNCTIONS IN SQL?
--------------------------
Functions in SQL are used to perform operations on data and return a result.
Functions help in:
  • Calculations
  • Data formatting
  • String operations
  • Date operations
  • Aggregate calculations

================================================================================
TYPES OF SQL FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

SQL functions are mainly divided into:
  1. Single Row Functions
  2. Aggregate Functions

================================================================================
1. SINGLE ROW FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

Single row functions work on one row at a time and return one result for each row.

Types:
  • String Functions
  • Number Functions
  • Date Functions
  • Conversion Functions

================================================================================
2. AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

Aggregate functions work on multiple rows and return one result.
Examples: COUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MAX(), MIN()

================================================================================
STRING FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

Used to manipulate text values.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPPER() - Converts text to uppercase

Syntax:
  SELECT UPPER(column_name) FROM table_name;

Example:
  SELECT UPPER(emp_name) FROM Employees;

Output:
  RAVI
  PRIYA

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOWER() - Converts text to lowercase

Example:
  SELECT LOWER(emp_name) FROM Employees;

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LENGTH() - Returns length of string

Example:
  SELECT LENGTH(emp_name) FROM Employees;

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONCAT() - Combines strings

Example:
  SELECT CONCAT(first_name, last_name) FROM Employees;

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUBSTRING() - Extracts part of string

Syntax:
  SELECT SUBSTRING(column_name, start, length) FROM table_name;

Example:
  SELECT SUBSTRING('DATABASE', 1, 4);

Output:
  DATA

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TRIM() - Removes spaces from beginning and end

Example:
  SELECT TRIM('   SQL   ');

Output:
  SQL

String Functions Execution Example (UPPER('sql')):
  Step 1: Take input string -> "sql"
  Step 2: Convert each character to uppercase -> "SQL"
  Step 3: Return result.

================================================================================
NUMBER FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

Used for numeric calculations.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ROUND() - Rounds number to specified decimals

Example:
  SELECT ROUND(45.678, 2);

Output:
  45.68

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CEIL() / CEILING() - Returns smallest integer >= value

Example:
  SELECT CEIL(45.2);

Output:
  46

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FLOOR() - Returns largest integer <= value

Example:
  SELECT FLOOR(45.9);

Output:
  45

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MOD() - Returns remainder

Example:
  SELECT MOD(10, 3);

Output:
  1

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ABS() - Returns absolute positive value

Example:
  SELECT ABS(-20);

Output:
  20

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SQRT() - Returns square root

Example:
  SELECT SQRT(25);

Output:
  5

Number Function Execution Example (ROUND(12.567, 2)):
  Step 1: Take number -> 12.567
  Step 2: Check decimal position.
  Step 3: Round value -> 12.57

================================================================================
DATE FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

Used to work with dates and time.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOW() - Returns current date and time

Example:
  SELECT NOW();

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CURDATE() - Returns current date

Example:
  SELECT CURDATE();

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CURTIME() - Returns current time

Example:
  SELECT CURTIME();

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATEDIFF() - Returns difference between two dates

Example:
  SELECT DATEDIFF('2026-06-01', '2026-05-20');

Output:
  12

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATE_ADD() - Adds days/months/years to date

Example:
  SELECT DATE_ADD('2026-05-29', INTERVAL 5 DAY);

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
YEAR(), MONTH(), DAY() - Extract parts of date

Example:
  SELECT YEAR('2026-05-29');

Output:
  2026

Date Function Execution Example (YEAR('2026-05-29')):
  Step 1: Take date.
  Step 2: Extract year portion -> 2026
  Step 3: Return result.

================================================================================
CONVERSION FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

Used to convert one datatype to another.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CAST() - Converts datatype

Syntax:
  SELECT CAST(value AS datatype);

Example:
  SELECT CAST(25.78 AS INT);

Output:
  25

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONVERT() - Another conversion function

Example:
  SELECT CONVERT('2026-05-29', DATE);

================================================================================
AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

Aggregate functions operate on multiple rows.

COUNT() - Counts rows
  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Employees;

SUM() - Calculates total
  SELECT SUM(salary) FROM Employees;

AVG() - Returns average
  SELECT AVG(salary) FROM Employees;

MAX() - Returns highest value
  SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employees;

MIN() - Returns lowest value
  SELECT MIN(salary) FROM Employees;

================================================================================
DIFFERENCE BETWEEN SINGLE ROW AND AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS
================================================================================

+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Single Row Functions     | Aggregate Functions      |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Work on one row          | Work on multiple rows    |
| Return one result per row| Return one result        |
| Example: UPPER()         | Example: SUM()           |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

================================================================================
SQL FUNCTION EXECUTION ORDER (EXAMPLE)
================================================================================

Query:
  SELECT department, AVG(salary)
  FROM Employees
  GROUP BY department;

Execution Flow:
  Step 1 (FROM)      → Read Employees table
  Step 2 (GROUP BY)  → Create groups
  Step 3 (AVG)       → Calculate average
  Step 4 (SELECT)    → Display output

================================================================================
IMPORTANT RULES
================================================================================

1. Functions simplify calculations.
2. Aggregate functions ignore NULL values.
3. String functions work on text data.
4. Date functions work on date/time values.
5. Conversion functions change datatypes.

